  <p class="wave-title">About</p>

  <div class="about-text">

    <p>
      The Earth is not still. Beneath the surface, tectonic plates shift, collide, and fracture,
      producing waves of energy that ripple across the planet. This project visualizes those
      movements as a living system. Something closer to a pulse than a map.
    </p>

    <p>
     Focusing on regions along the Pacific Ocean Ring of Fire, where 90% of seismic activity occurs, 
     the work translates ocean wave and earthquake data into continuous motion. 
     Ocean conditions are drawn from NOAA marine data, capturing real-time measurements of wave height and surface activity, 
     while seismic data is sourced from the USGS, reflecting shifts within the Earth itself.
    </p>

    <p>
        Rather than presenting raw data, 
        this project explores how environmental systems can be experienced visually, as rhythm, fluctuation, and signal. 
        What emerges is not just information, but a sense of the planet as something alive.
    </p>

  </div>

